<article class="post hentry">
<a href="/en/blog-details.html">
</a>
<header class="entry-header">
</header>
<div class="entry-content">
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/lhkh1.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: nld.com.vn</div>
</div>
<h3>Introductory Article</h3>
<h4>• Introduction</h4>
<p>
    The Khai Ha – Cau An Festival consists of four main parts: lowering the Neu tree, opening the year, first writing, and seal opening. <br/>
After the lowering of the Neu tree (previously, on the 29th day of the lunar year, after offering rituals to the deities at the courtyard of the Mausoleum, the raising of the Neu tree takes place to mark the end of the old year and the arrival of spring), the Ritual Committee conducts the Khai Ha ceremony, bringing offerings into the main temple, burning incense and offering wine, and reading prayers to honor the ancestors and wish for national peace and prosperity. <br/>
Next is the first writing ceremony of the new year, during which participants write auspicious words and wishes for a smooth and prosperous year. <br/>
The final part is the seal opening ceremony of Marshal Le Van Duyet, featuring the four characters “Tả quân chi ấn” (Seal of the Left Marshal). The Ritual Committee prepares calligraphy works bearing characters such as Fortune (Phúc), Longevity (Thọ), and Virtue (Đức), which are then stamped with the seal and presented to individuals who have made notable contributions to the community and society. <br/>
In addition to the traditional rituals, the festival also includes various cultural activities such as photo exhibitions, calligraphy demonstrations, folk games, and art performances, helping to preserve and promote the rich cultural heritage of Southern Vietnam among locals and visitors alike.
  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/lhkh2.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: phunuonline.com.vn</div>
</div>
<h4>• Origins</h4>
<p>
    The Khai Ha – Cau An Festival originated during the time when Marshal Lê Văn Duyệt served as the Governor of Gia Định Citadel. <br/>He chose the seventh day of the Lunar New Year as the Khai Ha Day, marking the end of the festive celebrations of Tet (Lunar New Year) and the return of people to their daily lives.
  </p>
<div class="post-frame">
<img alt="Post image" src="/images/lhkh3.jpg"/>
<div class="img-ref">Nguồn: qdnd.vn</div>
</div>
<h4>• Activities:</h4>
<p>
    The Khai Ha – Cau An Festival consists of four main parts: lowering the Neu tree, opening the year, first writing, and seal opening. <br/>
After the lowering of the Neu tree (previously, on the 29th day of the lunar year, after performing the ritual to worship the deities in the courtyard of the Mausoleum, the raising of the Neu tree takes place to mark the end of the old year and the arrival of spring), the Ritual Committee conducts the Khai Ha ceremony, bringing offerings into the main hall, burning incense, offering wine, and reading prayers to honor the ancestors and pray for peace and prosperity for the nation. <br/>
Following these two ceremonies is the first writing of the year, during which people write auspicious words and wishes for a smooth, prosperous year ahead. <br/>
Finally comes the seal opening ceremony of Marshal Lê Văn Duyệt, featuring the four characters “Tả quân chi ấn” (“Seal of the Left Marshal”). The Ritual Committee prepares calligraphy sheets with characters such as Phúc (Happiness), Thọ (Longevity), and Đức (Virtue), which are then stamped with the seal and presented to those who have made meaningful contributions to the community. <br/>
In addition to the traditional rituals, the festival also features photo exhibitions, calligraphy displays, folk games, and cultural performances, creating a lively and meaningful atmosphere that celebrates the spirit and traditions of Southern Vietnam.
  </p>
<h4>• Cultural and Historical Values:</h4>
<p>
    The Khai Ha – Cau An Festival at Lang Ong Ba Chieu is not only a spiritual and cultural event but also an occasion for people to express their gratitude and honor the contributions of Marshal Lê Văn Duyệt, an important historical figure of Southern Vietnam. <br/>
The festival embodies the traditional cultural spirit of the Southern people, reflecting their unity, respect for their roots, and aspiration for a peaceful and prosperous life. <br/>
In 2022, the Khai Ha – Cau An Festival at the Tomb of Marshal Lê Văn Duyệt was officially recognized by the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism as a National Intangible Cultural Heritage of Vietnam.
  </p>
</div>
</article>
